The '''History of Sarta (1530–1609)''' covers the period after the [[Convention of Paga]] and before the establishment of Sartan Guajicutea in 1609. It was marked by postwar consolidation, the growth of towns and institutions of learning, the expansion of Sartan political identity, and increasing maritime activity. In the final decades of the period, the [[War of the Mesogean]] created an opening for Sarta to acquire influence in [[Guajicutea]], ending with the formation of a new Sartan colony in 1609.

== After the Convention of Paga ==

The [[Convention of Paga]] in 1530 ended the principal phase of the [[Two Centuries War]] and marked the beginning of a more stable political order. Sarta emerged from the conflict as a more institutionalised kingdom than it had been in the early medieval period. The crown retained its alliances with [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]] and the papal states, but its rulers also faced a kingdom that was more urban, more literate, and more conscious of its own identity than in earlier centuries.

The end of the long war did not remove confessional politics from public life. Petran and Zamarran alignments continued to shape court politics, university teaching, and church administration. However, the cessation of large-scale frontier warfare allowed the crown to turn more attention toward administration, education, trade, and maritime ventures.

== State consolidation ==

The postwar kingdom relied on stronger administrative routines than earlier Sartan polities had required. Taxation, record-keeping, and service obligations became more regularised, especially in the larger towns and port districts. The crown increasingly depended on literate officials, legal clerks, and ecclesiastical administrators to manage the kingdom's obligations and alliances.

This period also saw the growth of royal and municipal institutions. Chartered towns gained greater importance in collecting revenues and coordinating trade, while noble households and church offices developed more formal procedures for landholding, inheritance, and service. The result was a more bureaucratic kingdom, even where local elites retained substantial autonomy.

== Universities and learning ==

The crown issued edicts to support higher learning and clerical training. Universities were founded or reorganised to train jurists, clerics, physicians, and administrators. These institutions helped standardise legal practice and ecclesiastical learning, and they gave the kingdom a durable intellectual elite.

University culture contributed to a more explicit Sartan identity. Chronicles, legal commentaries, and sermons increasingly described Sarta as a distinct historical community with its own political traditions. Education also strengthened the position of towns, where educated clerks and teachers became a visible part of civic life.

== Sartan identity ==

During this period, Sartan identity became more self-conscious in political writing and public institutions. Earlier Sartan-speaking communities had shared language and related elite traditions, but the long confessional conflict and the growth of the crown's institutions encouraged writers to define Sarta as a single political people.

This identity was expressed through law, church practice, and historical writing. Royal correspondence, university teaching, and local chronicles increasingly presented the kingdom as a unified realm rather than a loose collection of highland and lowland territories. The process did not eliminate regional difference, but it gave those differences a common political frame.

== Coastal and mountain Sarta ==

The contrast between western and eastern Sarta became more visible after 1530. The western and coastal regions were more closely tied to shipping, exchange, and foreign contact. They developed stronger links to [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]] and to the wider maritime world of southern Illypnia. Coastal towns tended to be commercially oriented and more exposed to outside influence.

The eastern and mountainous regions developed differently. They remained tied to fortresses, inland routes, and defensive landholding. Mountain communities and inland nobles often retained a more conservative outlook and a stronger attachment to older forms of authority. The divide was not absolute, but it became an important feature of Sartan society and politics.

== Maritime expansion ==

After the Convention of Paga, Sarta showed increasing interest in maritime ventures. The kingdom sponsored exploratory voyages, trade probes, and limited attempts to establish overseas footholds. Some ventures produced useful information or short-lived commercial contacts, but most did not result in permanent colonies.

This failure to establish an early overseas empire shaped later policy. Sarta's seaborne activity continued to grow, but it remained constrained by the larger naval reach of other Illypnian powers, especially [[Volisania]]. The crown nevertheless treated maritime knowledge as an important asset, and shipbuilding and coastal navigation became more closely tied to state interests.

== Relations with Volisania ==

Sarta's relationship with [[Volisania]] remained important throughout the period. The papal alliance had been strengthened during the [[Two Centuries War]], but the practical balance of costs and authority remained uneven. Sartan nobles, sailors, and merchants increasingly resented what they saw as a relationship in which Sarta bore a greater share of the burdens while Volisania retained greater diplomatic distance from the front.

That resentment did not produce immediate rupture. Instead, it formed part of a growing reserve toward Volisanian authority and contributed to the willingness of Sartan leaders to pursue independent diplomacy when the opportunity arose.

== War of the Mesogean and Guajicutea ==

The [[War of the Mesogean]] between [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]] created a major opportunity for Sarta. The war was fought primarily in the Old World, while the Capuyaquiran remained a secondary theatre of naval harassment and ship impressment. In [[Guajicutea]], both colonial powers reduced their attention as the war continued, and Sarta moved to exploit the resulting opening.

In 1600, Sarta sent an emissary to [[Tabakurí]] to establish formal relations. After the hurricane of 1601, Sarta provided substantial aid, which strengthened its influence on the island. In 1605, Sarta and Guajicutea signed a formal alliance. In 1607, Sarta purchased the Burawi fort at [[Waribá]], removing the last meaningful Burawi military position on the island. In 1608, Sarta negotiated a neutrality arrangement with Volisania, which led to Volisanian withdrawal from Guajicutea. By late 1608, Sarta was the only external power with an effective presence on the island.

The brief [[Sarta-Guajicutea War]] followed in 1608–1609. Sartan forces captured Tabakurí, deposed [[Boqor Carlos I]], and established the [[Sarta Colony of Guajicutea]] in 1609. The new colony ended Guaji kingship and replaced the remaining local feudal order with Sartan colonial administration.

== Convention and aftermath ==

The formation of Sartan Guajicutea marked the end of the period. By 1609, Sarta had moved beyond postwar consolidation into overseas possession. The kingdom had become more institutionalised at home, more outward-looking at sea, and more willing to use diplomacy, commerce, and force in combination.

The era left Sarta with a stronger bureaucracy, a larger educated class, more developed coastal institutions, and a more pronounced distinction between its coastal and mountainous regions. It also left the kingdom more deeply involved in overseas affairs, setting the stage for the colonial period that followed.

